<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- The single source of truth for the NSServices declaration. Merged
       into target/Aloud.app's bundle Info.plist by packaging/make-app.sh
       (PlistBuddy `Merge`) — NOT by the Tauri CLI bundler, which this
       project deliberately does not use for macOS packaging (see
       CLAUDE.md's Stack section for why). Keep this file containing only
       what make-app.sh should merge wholesale into the bundle plist.

       NSMessage is the selector STEM: "readSelection" means macOS invokes
       -readSelection:userData:error: on the registered services provider.
       If the two ever disagree the Service silently never fires — no error,
       no log. Keep this string and the #[unsafe(method(...))] attribute in
       src/selection/macos.rs in lockstep.

       NSSendTypes MUST be a UTI (public.utf8-plain-text), not the legacy
       NSStringPboardType: modern macOS filters the Services menu by UTI,
       so a legacy pasteboard type here means the entry silently never
       shows up in Services -> Read Aloud, with no error anywhere. Verified
       on the built bundle 2026-08-09 (pbs -dump_pboard) before this file
       was fixed to match.

       NSKeyEquivalent's "default" value uses NSMenuItem's modifier-mask
       shorthand, one character per modifier, prefixed to the key:
       @ = Command, $ = Shift, ~ = Option, ^ = Control. "^@s" is
       Ctrl+Cmd+S. This is the ONLY way to give a Service a default
       shortcut without Accessibility permission — see README.md
       "Permissions" for why Accessibility is avoided entirely. The user
       can override this (or clear it) in System Settings -> Keyboard ->
       Keyboard Shortcuts -> Services.

       WHY Ctrl+Cmd+S, changed 2026-08-16 from "$@a" (Cmd+Shift+A).
       Cmd+Shift+A collided with TWO things from the very first commit,
       and shipped that way for a week:

         - Terminal's built-in "Search man Page Index in Terminal"
           Service, whose NSKeyEquivalent is the bare letter "A" (read
           back live with `/System/Library/CoreServices/pbs
           -dump_pboard`). Two Services claiming one chord; which wins is
           undefined. Its NSRequiredContext is NSWordLimit=20, so it only
           competes for SHORT selections — which is why the failure
           looked intermittent rather than total.
         - Google Chrome's own "Search Tabs" menu item. An app's own key
           equivalent beats a Service's, so in Chrome the Service was
           simply unreachable by chord (the Services *menu* still worked).

       Ctrl+Cmd was chosen as a family, not just a letter, and checked
       rather than assumed: macOS claims no Ctrl+Cmd+letter system-wide
       (`defaults read com.apple.symbolichotkeys` — every Control entry
       is a function key or arrow), and the Claude desktop app's entire
       accelerator table contains no Ctrl+Cmd binding at all. Chrome's
       Mac shortcuts live in Cmd / Shift+Cmd / Opt+Cmd and it does not
       use Ctrl+Cmd. Verified working in Chrome, Notes and Claude before
       shipping.

       The letter A was deliberately abandoned rather than re-modified:
       Ctrl+Shift+A ALSO reached Terminal's man-page Service, which a
       strict modifier match does not explain. Rather than bet the app's
       main gesture on a mechanism we do not model correctly, the letter
       moved too. S is for Speak.

       NOTE the default is only a default. A user override lives in
       NSServicesStatus in ~/Library/Preferences/pbs.plist and WINS over
       this file, and macOS never notifies the app that it changed — so
       anything rendering this chord in the UI must read the live value,
       not hardcode it. -->
  <key>NSServices</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>NSMenuItem</key>
      <dict><key>default</key><string>Read Aloud</string></dict>
      <!-- NSRequiredContext must be PRESENT even when empty. Without it macOS
           silently omits the service from the Services menu — no error, no log.
           An empty dict means "offer this service in every context". -->
      <key>NSRequiredContext</key>
      <dict/>
      <key>NSReturnTypes</key>
      <array/>
      <key>NSMessage</key>
      <string>readSelection</string>
      <key>NSPortName</key>
      <string>Aloud</string>
      <key>NSSendTypes</key>
      <array><string>public.utf8-plain-text</string></array>
      <key>NSKeyEquivalent</key>
      <dict><key>default</key><string>^@s</string></dict>
    </dict>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
